      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate carefully.\n\nThis is an OIML Certificate of Conformity from the Czech Metrology Institute (ČMI):\n- Certificate No. R49/2006-CZ-11.01, Revision 1\n- Issued 19 December 2011\n- For R 49, edition 2006, accuracy class 1 and 2\n- Magnetic Flow Meter: Transmitter type 8732 and Flow Sensor types 8705 and 8711\n- Applicant/Manufacturer: Emerson Process Management / Rosemount Flow Division\n\nThe certificate includes several tables organized by sensor type and DN (nominal diameter):\n\n**Table 1 (Page 3):** 8732 + 8711 (wafer), PTFE liner, DN 4, 8, 15, 25, 40\n**Table 2 (Page 4):** 8732 + 8711 (wafer), PTFE liner, DN 50, 65, 80, 100, 150, 200\n**Table 3 (Page 5):** 8732 + 8705 (flanges), Neoprene/Polyurethane/PTFE liner, DN 15, 25, 40, 50, 80\n**Table 4 (Page 6):** 8732 + 8705 (flanges), DN 100, 150, 200, 250, 300, 350\n**Table 5 (Page 7):** 8732 + 8705 (flanges), DN 400, 450, 500, 600, 750, 900\n\nWait, let me check page 4 again. Page 3 says \"Magnetic Flow Meter Transmitter types 8732 and Magnetic Flow Sensor types 8711 with the lining material made by PTFE\" — the table on page 3 and page 4 both belong to the 8711 sensor. Page 3 continues \"Further characteristics see page 3\" — hmm, actually page 1 says \"Further characteristics see page 3\". The table on page 3 is for 8711 sensor (wafer connection type per the table). Page 4's table also has \"Connection type: Wafer\", so it continues the 8711 series with larger DN sizes. Page 5 says \"Magnetic Flow Meter Transmitter types 8732 and Magnetic Flow Sensor types 8705 with the lining material made by Neoprene, Polyurethane and PTFE\" — connection type \"Standard flanges\". Pages 6 and 7 continue the 8705 tables.\n\nSo the models are DN sizes across two sensor types.\n\nLet me structure this:\n\nModels: The certificate covers the transmitter 8732 combined with sensor 8705 or 8711, each at various DN sizes.\n\nI'll define model_id per combination: e.g., \"8711-DN4\", \"8711-DN8\", ... and \"8705-DN15\", etc.\n\nComponents:\n- Transmitter (measuring transducer / electronic calculator): type 8732\n- Flow sensor: types 8705 and 8711 (alternatives)\n\nActually, per the description: \"The water meters consist of flow sensor types 8705 and 8711 and of measuring transducer with the electronic calculator type 8732.\"\n\nComponents:\n1. role: \"measuring transducer / electronic calculator (transmitter)\", type_designations: [\"8732\"]\n2. role: \"flow sensor\", type_designations: [\"8705\", \"8711\"], alternatives: \"OR\"\n\nNow characteristics. There are 5 tables. Each table has rows of characteristics varying by DN. I should produce matrix_tables for each table, plus model_level characteristics.\n\nGiven the complexity, let me build matrix_tables with rows per DN and columns for the varying attributes, and also populate model_level entries for the main varying attributes.\n\nLet me extract each table carefully.\n\n**Table 1 (page 3): 8732 + 8711, PTFE liner, Wafer**\n\nDN: 4, 8, 15, 25, 40\nQ4 (m³/h): ≤0.50, ≤2.0, ≤7.9, ≤20.0, ≤50.0\nQ3 (m³/h): ≤0.40, ≤1.6, ≤6.3, ≤16.0, ≤40.0 (footnote 1)\nQ2 (m³/h): ≥0.00512, ≥0.0205, ≥0.0504, ≥0.128, ≥0.320\nQ1 (m³/h): ≥0.00320, ≥0.0128, ≥0.0320, ≥0.080, ≥0.200\nQ3/Q1: ≤125 (DN 4, 8) footnote 2; ≤200 (DN 15, 25, 40) footnote 2\nQ2/Q1: 1.6\nQ4/Q3: 1.25\nAccuracy class: 2\nMPE lower: ±5%\nMPE upper: ±2% (T≤30°C), ±3% (T>30°C)\nTemperature class: T90 for PTFE liner\nMax admissible pressure [bar]: 0 to 19 (DN 4, 8, 15 — colspan 3), 0 to 50 (DN 25, 40 — colspan 2)\n\nWait, let me check the colspans: `<td>Maximum admissible pressure [bar]:</td><td colspan=\"3\">0 to 19</td><td colspan=\"2\">0 to 50</td>` — yes, first 3 columns (DN 4, 8, 15) are 0-19 bar, last 2 (DN 25, 40) are 0-50 bar.\n\nMax pressure-loss [bar]: 0.16\nEnvironmental classes: B and C\nElectromagnetic environment: E1 and E2\nIndicating range [m³]: 9 999 to 99 999 (i.e., 9999 to 99999)\nResolution of indicating device [L]: 0.01 (DN 4, 8), 0.1 (DN 15, 25), 1 (DN 40)\nMin straight length inlet: 5\nMin straight length outlet: 3\nOrientation: Arbitrary\nLength of water meter [mm]: 55, 55, 56, 57, 73\nConnection type: Wafer\nPower supply AC: Umax 250 V, Umin 90 V, frequency 50 or 60 Hz\nPower supply DC: Umax 42 V, Umin 12 V\n\n**Table 2 (page 4): 8732 + 8711 continued, PTFE, Wafer**\n\nDN: 50, 65, 80, 100, 150, 200\nQ4: ≤78.8, ≤125, ≤200, ≤313, ≤788, ≤1250\nQ3: ≤63.0, ≤100, ≤160, ≤250, ≤630, ≤1000 (fn 1)\nQ2: ≥0.504, ≥1.60, ≥2.56, ≥4.0, ≥10.1, ≥16.0\nQ1: ≥0.315, ≥1.00, ≥1.60, ≥2.5, ≥6.3, ≥10.0\nQ3/Q1: ≤100 (all, fn 2)\nQ2/Q1: 1.6\nQ4/Q3: 1.25\nAccuracy class: 2 (DN 50), 1 (rest)\nMPE lower: ±5% (DN 50), ±3% (rest)\nMPE upper: ≤30°C: ±2% (DN 50), ±1% (rest); >30°C: ±3% (DN 50), ±2% (rest)\nTemperature classes: T90 PTFE\nEnvironmental: B and C\nEMC: E1 and E2\nMax admissible pressure: 0 to 50 bar (all)\nMax pressure loss: 0.16 bar\nIndicating range [m³]: 999 999 to 9 999 999\nResolution [L]: 1 (DN 50-150, colspan 5), 10 (DN 200)\n\nWait, colspan 5 covers DN 50, 65, 80, 100, 150 → resolution 1 L; last col DN 200 → 10 L.\n\nMin straight inlet: 5, outlet: 3\nLength [mm]: 84 (DN50), 120 (DN65, 80 — colspan 2), 149 (DN100), 180 (DN150), 230 (DN200)\nConnection: Wafer\nPower: same as table 1.\n\n**Table 3 (page 5): 8732 + 8705, Neoprene/Polyurethane/PTFE, Standard flanges**\n\nDN: 15, 25, 40, 50, 80\nQ4: ≤7.9, ≤20.0, ≤50.0, ≤78.8, ≤200\nQ3: ≤6.3, ≤16.0, ≤40.0, ≤63.0, ≤160 (fn1)\nQ2: ≥0.0504, ≥0.128, ≥0.320, ≥0.504, ≥2.56\nQ1: ≥0.0320, ≥0.080, ≥0.200, ≥0.315, ≥1.60\nQ3/Q1: ≤200 (DN15-50, colspan 4, fn2), ≤100 (DN80, fn2)\nQ2/Q1: 1.6\nQ4/Q3: 1.25\nAccuracy class: 2 (DN15-50), 1 (DN80)\nMPE lower: ±5% (DN15-50), ±3% (DN80)\nMPE upper: ≤30°C: ±2% / ±1%; >30°C: ±3% / ±2%\nTemperature classes: T50 polyurethane, T70 neoprene, T90 PTFE\nEnvironmental: B and C\nEMC: E1 and E2\nMax admissible pressure: 0 to 50 bar\nMax pressure loss: 0.16 bar\nIndicating range [m³]: 99 999 to 999 999 (99999 to 999999)\nResolution [L]: 0.1 (DN15, 25), 1 (DN40, 50, 80)\nMin straight inlet: 5, outlet: 2\nLength [mm]: 200 all\nConnection: Standard flanges\nPower: same.\n\n**Table 4 (page 6): 8732 + 8705 continued**\n\nDN: 100, 150, 200, 250, 300, 350\nQ4: ≤313, ≤788, ≤1250, ≤2000, ≤3125, ≤3125\nQ3: ≤250, ≤630, ≤1000, ≤1600, ≤2500, ≤2500 (fn1)\nQ2: ≥4.0, ≥10.1, ≥16.0, ≥25.6, ≥40.0, ≥50.0\nQ1: ≥2.5, ≥6.3, ≥10.0, ≥16.0, ≥25.0, ≥31.3\nQ3/Q1: ≤100 (DN100-300, colspan 5, fn2), ≤80 (DN350, fn2)\nQ2/Q1: 1.6\nQ4/Q3: 1.25\nAccuracy class: 1 (all)\nMPE lower: ±3%\nMPE upper: ≤30°C: ±1%; >30°C: ±2%\nTemperature: T50 PU, T70 neoprene, T90 PTFE\nEnvironmental: B and C\nEMC: E1, E2\nMax admissible pressure: 0 to 50 bar\nMax pressure loss: 0.16 bar\nIndicating range [m³]: 999 999 to 9 999 999\nResolution [L]: 1 (DN100), 10 (DN150-300, colspan 4), 100 (DN350)\nMin straight inlet: 5, outlet: 2\nLength [mm]: 250, 300, 350, 435, 512, 588\nConnection: Standard flanges\nPower: same.\n\n**Table 5 (page 7): 8732 + 8705 continued**\n\nDN: 400, 450, 500, 600, 750, 900\nQ4: ≤3125, ≤3125, ≤5000, ≤5000, ≤7875, ≤12500\nQ3: ≤2500, ≤2500, ≤4000, ≤4000, ≤6300, ≤10000 (fn1)\nQ2: ≥63.5, ≥80.0, ≥101.6, ≥128.0, ≥201.6, ≥320.0\nQ1: ≥39.7, ≥50.0, ≥63.5, ≥80.0, ≥126.0, ≥200.0\nQ3/Q1: ≤63 (DN400, fn2), ≤50 (DN450, fn2), ≤63 (DN500, fn2), ≤50 (DN600, 750, 900, colspan 3, fn2)\nQ2/Q1: 1.6\nQ4/Q3: 1.25\nAccuracy class: 1\nMPE lower: ±3%\nMPE upper: ≤30°C ±1%; >30°C ±2%\nTemperature: T50 PU, T70 neoprene, T90 PTFE\nEnvironmental: B and C\nEMC: E1, E2\nMax admissible pressure: 0 to 40 bar (note: 40, not 50, for this page!)\nMax pressure loss: 0.16 bar\nIndicating range [m³]: 9 999 999 (probably up to 9 999 999)\nResolution [L]: 100\nMin straight inlet: 5, outlet: 2\nLength [mm]: 664, 761, 839, 1000, 1200, 1200\nConnection: Standard flanges\nPower: same.\n\nFootnotes:\n1) The value of Q3 shall be chosen from the R5 line of ISO 3:1973.\n2) The ratio Q3/Q1 shall be chosen from the R10 line from ISO 3:1973 and this value shall be higher than 10.\n\nNow let me plan the output:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2006-CZ-11.01\",\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",  // hmm, is it stated? Not stated. OIML-CS certificates... R49 2006 certificates are typically under the old OIML certificate system, scheme A wasn't defined then. I should omit or null. The schema says scheme (A|B). Not present in the document — omit it per rule 8.\n    \"project_number\": null,\n    \"page_total\": 7,\n    \"member_state\": \"Czech Republic\",\n    \"date_issued\": \"2011-12-19\",\n    \"oiml_issuer_id\": null\n  },\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nRevision history: \"Revision 1\" — the certificate has revision 1. Revision history entries: revision \"1\"? The schema has revision_history[]: revision (string), date, changes. The markdown only shows \"Revision 1\" without date/changes. Hmm. I could include {\"revision\": \"1\", \"date\": \"2011-12-19\"}? Actually the date is the issue date. It's risky to fabricate. I'll include revision_history with revision \"1\" and date \"2011-12-19\" and no changes? Actually rule 8 says don't fabricate. The date 19 December 2011 is the date of signatures, which could be associated with the revision. I think it's reasonable to include {\"revision\": \"1\", \"date\": \"2011-12-19\"}. Or omit changes. Let me include it.\n\nTest reports: No. 6015-PT-P0076-11 (41 pages plus annexes) and No. 6015-PT-P0157-11 (39 pages plus annexes). Roles: type_evaluation_report. Pages: 41 and 39.\n\nRecommendation: id \"R49\", edition 2006, accuracy_classes [1, 2].\n\nApplicants: Emerson Process Management / Rosemount Flow Division, address 12001 Technology Drive, Eden Prairie, 553 44 MN, USA.\n\nManufacturers: same.\n\nIssuing authority: Czech Metrology Institute, Okružní 31, 638 00 Brno, CZ. Person responsible: Jan Kalandra. \n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Water meter\" — the certificate says \"Magnetic Flow Meter\" and describes \"water meters\". R49 is \"Water meters intended for the metering of cold potable water and hot potable water\". Category options in schema: \"Load cell\", \"Indicator\", \"Measuring system\", \"Fuel dispenser\", \"Taximeter\", \"Gas meter\", ... The instrument is a water meter / magnetic flow meter. I'll use \"Water meter\" (fits the R49 context; the description says water meters). Or \"Magnetic flow meter\". The schema allows free category. I'll use \"Water meter (magnetic flow meter)\"? Hmm, better keep it simple: \"Water meter\". Actually the document titles it \"Magnetic Flow Meter\". The recommendation R49 is water meters. The description repeatedly calls them \"water meters\". I'll go with \"Water meter\".\n- type_designations: [\"8732\", \"8705\", \"8711\"] — all type identifiers mentioned. The title: \"Type: Transmitter type 8732 and Flow Sensor types 8705 and 8711\".\n- module_designation: null or \"Not applicable\"? Not stated. Omit/null.\n- description: from page 2 description.\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: null\n- models: one per DN per sensor type. Models: \n  - 8711: DN 4, 8, 15, 25, 40, 50, 65, 80, 100, 150, 200 (11 models)\n  - 8705: DN 15, 25, 40, 50, 80, 100, 150, 200, 250, 300, 350, 400, 450, 500, 600, 750, 900 (17 models)\n  Total 28 models.\n\nmodel_id naming: \"8711 DN4\"? I'll use \"8711-DN4\" etc. Labels could include sensor type + DN. attributes per model could include liner options and connection type:\n- 8711 models: connection wafer, liner PTFE\n- 8705 models: connection standard flanges, liner neoprene/polyurethane/PTFE\n\nActually wait — for 8705, tables 3-5 mention lining \"made by Neoprene, Polyurethane and PTFE\". Temperature classes T50 for polyurethane, T70 for neoprene, T90 for PTFE. So the liner is a configuration axis. For 8711, only PTFE (T90).\n\nI could model liner as config_level axis \"liner_material\" with conditions \"polyurethane\" → temperature_class T50, \"neoprene\" → T70, \"PTFE\" → T90. That fits config_level.\n\nmodel_level characteristics: The main matrix is flowrates per DN. Since models are sensor-type+DN combos, model_level entries would have 28 values each. That's a lot but doable. Attributes:\n- nominal_dn (mm)\n- overload_flowrate_q4 (m³/h) — upper bounds\n- permanent_flowrate_q3 (m³/h) — upper bounds (fn 1)\n- transitional_flowrate_q2 (m³/h) — lower bounds\n- minimum_flowrate_q1 (m³/h) — lower bounds\n- max_admissible_pressure (bar) — varies: 0-19 (8711 DN4,8,15), 0-50 (8711 DN25-200, 8705 DN15-350), 0-40 (8705 DN400-900)\n- length_of_water_meter (mm)\n- resolution_of_indicating_device (L)\n- accuracy_class (dimensionless) — varies by DN\n- q3_over_q1 ratio — varies (≤125, ≤200, ≤100, ≤80, ≤63, ≤50)\n- mpe values vary with accuracy class.\n\nThat's a lot of entries. Let me be systematic but maybe focus on the key flowrate attributes in model_level, and capture full tables in matrix_tables. The schema says model_level is \"attribute × model matrix\". I'll include: nominal_dn, q4, q3, q2, q1, accuracy_class, length, resolution, max_admissible_pressure, q3_q1_ratio. Hmm, that's 10 attributes × 28 models each. That's verbose but it's the structured representation. Let me decide how much to include. The instructions emphasize matrix detection being MOST IMPORTANT. So I should do it properly.\n\nAlternatively, I can put per-model attributes in model_family.models[].attributes{} — that might be more compact: each model with attributes dict. The schema supports both: models[].attributes{} and characteristics.model_level[]. Hmm. Doing both would be extremely verbose. Let me think about what's best.\n\nGiven the schema design, I think:\n- model_family.models: list of 28 models with attributes containing key per-model values (dn, sensor_type, q3, q4, q1, q2, accuracy_class, length, connection, liner).\n- characteristics.model_level: a few key attributes.\n\nHmm, but duplicating everything twice is wasteful. The R137 example in the instructions shows models[] without attributes and model_level with the attributes. Let me follow that pattern: models[] just model_ids, and model_level carries the matrices.\n\nBut wait — for values like \"≤ 0.50\" (Q4 upper bound) and \"≥ 0.00512\" (Q1 lower bound), how to encode value? Per rule 2, ranges become {min, max}. \"≤0.50\" → {min: null, max: 0.5}. Hmm, in the flowrate context, Q4 is a maximum: the overload flowrate is at most 0.50 m³/h. So value = {min: null, max: 0.5}. Similarly Q1 ≥ 0.00320 → {min: 0.0032, max: null}.\n\nHmm, but these flowrates are really defined points; the ≤/≥ indicate limits for the meter's ranges. The certificate lists max Q4, Q3 and min Q2, Q1. I'll encode as {min: null, max: X} for Q4/Q3 and {min: X, max: null} for Q2/Q1. That's faithful.\n\nNow, 28 models per attribute array — let me enumerate model IDs:\n\n8711 (wafer, PTFE):\n1. 8711-DN4\n2. 8711-DN8\n3. 8711-DN15\n4. 8711-DN25\n5. 8711-DN40\n6. 8711-DN50\n7. 8711-DN65\n8. 8711-DN80\n9. 8711-DN100\n10. 8711-DN150\n11. 8711-DN200\n\n8705 (flanged, PU/neoprene/PTFE):\n12. 8705-DN15\n13. 8705-DN25\n14. 8705-DN40\n15. 8705-DN50\n16. 8705-DN80\n17. 8705-DN100\n18. 8705-DN150\n19. 8705-DN200\n20. 8705-DN250\n21. 8705-DN300\n22. 8705-DN350\n23. 8705-DN400\n24. 8705-DN450\n25. 8705-DN500\n26. 8705-DN600\n27. 8705-DN750\n28. 8705-DN900\n\nNow model_level attributes:\n\n**nominal_dn** (mm): 8711: 4, 8, 15, 25, 40, 50, 65, 80, 100, 150, 200; 8705: 15, 25, 40, 50, 80, 100, 150, 200, 250, 300, 350, 400, 450, 500, 600, 750, 900.\n\n**overload_flowrate_q4** (m³/h), upper bound:\n- 8711-DN4: 0.5\n- 8711-DN8: 2.0\n- 8711-DN15: 7.9\n- 8711-DN25: 20.0\n- 8711-DN40: 50.0\n- 8711-DN50: 78.8\n- 8711-DN65: 125\n- 8711-DN80: 200\n- 8711-DN100: 313\n- 8711-DN150: 788\n- 8711-DN200: 1250\n- 8705-DN15: 7.9\n- 8705-DN25: 20.0\n- 8705-DN40: 50.0\n- 8705-DN50: 78.8\n- 8705-DN80: 200\n- 8705-DN100: 313\n- 8705-DN150: 788\n- 8705-DN200: 1250\n- 8705-DN250: 2000\n- 8705-DN300: 3125\n- 8705-DN350: 3125\n- 8705-DN400: 3125\n- 8705-DN450: 3125\n- 8705-DN500: 5000\n- 8705-DN600: 5000\n- 8705-DN750: 7875\n- 8705-DN900: 12500\n\n**permanent_flowrate_q3** (m³/h), upper bound, fn 1:\n- 8711: 0.4, 1.6, 6.3, 16.0, 40.0, 63.0, 100, 160, 250, 630, 1000\n- 8705: 6.3, 16.0, 40.0, 63.0, 160, 250, 630, 1000, 1600, 2500, 2500, 2500, 2500, 4000, 4000, 6300, 10000\n\n**transitional_flowrate_q2** (m³/h), lower bound:\n- 8711: 0.00512, 0.0205, 0.0504, 0.128, 0.320, 0.504, 1.60, 2.56, 4.0, 10.1, 16.0\n- 8705: 0.0504, 0.128, 0.320, 0.504, 2.56, 4.0, 10.1, 16.0, 25.6, 40.0, 50.0, 63.5, 80.0, 101.6, 128.0, 201.6, 320.0\n\n**minimum_flowrate_q1** (m³/h), lower bound:\n- 8711: 0.00320, 0.0128, 0.0320, 0.080, 0.200, 0.315, 1.00, 1.60, 2.5, 6.3, 10.0\n- 8705: 0.0320, 0.080, 0.200, 0.315, 1.60, 2.5, 6.3, 10.0, 16.0, 25.0, 31.3, 39.7, 50.0, 63.5, 80.0, 126.0, 200.0\n\n**q3_over_q1 ratio (max)**, fn 2:\n- 8711-DN4: 125\n- 8711-DN8: 125\n- 8711-DN15: 200\n- 8711-DN25: 200\n- 8711-DN40: 200\n- 8711-DN50..200: 100\n- 8705-DN15..50: 200\n- 8705-DN80..300: 100\n- 8705-DN350: 80\n- 8705-DN400: 63\n- 8705-DN450: 50\n- 8705-DN500: 63\n- 8705-DN600: 50\n- 8705-DN750: 50\n- 8705-DN900: 50\n\n**accuracy_class**:\n- 8711-DN4..40: 2\n- 8711-DN50: 2\n- 8711-DN65..200: 1\n- 8705-DN15..50: 2\n- 8705-DN80..900: 1\n\n**maximum_admissible_pressure** (bar) range:\n- 8711-DN4: 0-19\n- 8711-DN8: 0-19\n- 8711-DN15: 0-19\n- 8711-DN25: 0-50\n- 8711-DN40: 0-50\n- 8711-DN50..200: 0-50\n- 8705-DN15..350: 0-50\n- 8705-DN400..900: 0-40\n\n**length_of_water_meter** (mm):\n- 8711: 55, 55, 56, 57, 73, 84, 120, 120, 149, 180, 230\n- 8705: 200, 200, 200, 200, 200, 250, 300, 350, 435, 512, 588, 664, 761, 839, 1000, 1200, 1200\n\n**resolution_of_indicating_device** (L):\n- 8711-DN4: 0.01\n- 8711-DN8: 0.01\n- 8711-DN15: 0.1\n- 8711-DN25: 0.1\n- 8711-DN40: 1\n- 8711-DN50: 1\n- 8711-DN65: 1\n- 8711-DN80: 1\n- 8711-DN100: 1\n- 8711-DN150: 1\n- 8711-DN200: 10\n- 8705-DN15: 0.1\n- 8705-DN25: 0.1\n- 8705-DN40: 1\n- 8705-DN50: 1\n- 8705-DN80: 1\n- 8705-DN100: 1\n- 8705-DN150: 10\n- 8705-DN200: 10\n- 8705-DN250: 10\n- 8705-DN300: 10\n- 8705-DN350: 100\n- 8705-DN400..900: 100\n\nWait — resolution row in table 4 (page 6): `<td>1</td><td colspan=\"4\">10</td><td>100</td>` — DN100: 1, DN150-300: 10 (colspan 4 = 150, 200, 250, 300), DN350: 100. Yes, matches what I wrote.\n\nTable 2 (page 4): `<td colspan=\"5\">1</td><td>10</td>` — DN50-150: 1, DN200: 10. Yes.\n\nTable 3 (page 5): `<td colspan=\"2\">0.1</td><td colspan=\"3\">1</td>` — DN15, 25: 0.1; DN40, 50, 80: 1. Yes.\n\nTable 1 (page 3): `<td colspan=\"2\">0.01</td><td colspan=\"2\">0.1</td><td>1</td>` — DN4, 8: 0.01; DN15, 25: 0.1; DN40: 1. Yes.\n\n**indicating_range** (m³) varies by table:\n- 8711-DN4..40: 9999 to 99999\n- 8711-DN50..200: 999999 to 9999999\n- 8705-DN15..80: 99999 to 999999\n- 8705-DN100..350: 999999 to 9999999\n- 8705-DN400..900: \"9 999 999\" — indicating range given as single value \"9 999 999\" — ambiguous; probably up to 9 999 999 m³. I'll put {min: null, max: 9999999} or just record max. Hmm. For simplicity, I might capture indicating range in matrix_tables rather than model_level. Or as a per-model attribute. Let me include it in model_level as range values.\n\nActually, this is getting huge. Let me reconsider: the matrix_tables preserve full tables; model_level captures varying attributes. I'll include the most important ones in model_level: nominal_dn, q4, q3, q2, q1, q3_q1_ratio, accuracy_class, max_admissible_pressure, length_of_water_meter, resolution_of_indicating_device, indicating_range. That's 11 attributes × 28 models = ~308 value objects. That's large but JSON is fine with it. Hmm, output length might be a concern but let's try to be complete yet efficient.\n\nAlternatively, use model_family.models[].attributes{} for per-model values — 28 models each with ~12 attributes — similar size. The schema prefers model_level for attribute×model. I'll do model_level.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider whether to encode Q3/Q1/Q2/Q4 as ranges. The ≤/≥ indicate that these are the maximum/minumum specified flowrates for that DN. The actual value chosen must be per ISO 3 R5 line (fn 1 for Q3). I think {min:null, max:0.5} for Q4 and {min:0.0032, max:null} for Q1 is the most faithful. Actually, hold on — maybe simpler: for Q4 and Q3 record max bound; for Q2 and Q1 record min bound. Using {min, max} objects with null for the missing side. The schema says value can be number or {min,max} or string. Using {min: null, max: 0.5} is per rule \"Open-ended ranges use null for the missing bound: ≥1000 → {min: 1000, max: null}\". Good.\n\nNow type_level characteristics (whole-cert):\n- q2_over_q1_ratio: 1.6 (dimensionless)\n- q4_over_q3_ratio: 1.25\n- mpe_lower_zone: ±5% — varies by accuracy class/DN though. For class 2 meters: 5%; class 1: 3%. Since accuracy class varies per model, MPE varies too. I could put mpe in config_level with axis \"accuracy_class\": condition \"1\" → lower ±3%, upper ≤30°C ±1%, >30°C ±2%; condition \"2\" → lower ±5%, upper ≤30°C ±2%, >30°C ±3%. That's a clean config_level representation!\n\nconfig_level:\n- attribute: \"mpe_lower_flowrate_zone\", axis: \"accuracy_class\", values: [{condition: \"1\", value: 3, unit_symbol: \"%\"}, {condition: \"2\", value: 5, unit_symbol: \"%\"}] (with ± implied; maybe value: \"±5\"? Rule: numbers not strings, but \"±\" — I could keep value: 5 and add sign in... hmm. The schema has value, unit_symbol, footnote_markers. No sign field. I'll use value \"±5\" as string? Or -5? MPE is ±. Common representation: value: \"±5\". Since ± isn't a plain number, string \"±5\" might be acceptable (\"Formulas/symbols\" rule allows strings). Hmm, but rule says numbers not strings where possible. I'll use string \"±5\" to preserve meaning. Actually maybe value: 5 with attribute name mpe_..._max_absolute? Cleaner: attribute \"maximum_permitted_error_lower_zone\", value 5, unit \"%\" — the ± is implied by \"maximum permissible error\". I'll go numeric 5 and 3, etc. Hmm, but losing \"±\" info. The term MPE inherently means ±. I'll go numeric.\n\n- attribute: \"mpe_upper_flowrate_zone_water_temp_le_30c\", axis: \"accuracy_class\", values: [{condition: \"1\", value: 1}, {condition: \"2\", value: 2}], unit \"%\"\n- attribute: \"mpe_upper_flowrate_zone_water_temp_gt_30c\", axis: \"accuracy_class\", values: [{condition: \"1\", value: 2}, {condition: \"2\", value: 3}], unit \"%\"\n\n- attribute: \"temperature_class\", axis: \"liner_material\", values: [{condition: \"polyurethane\", value: \"T50\"}, {condition: \"neoprene\", value: \"T70\"}, {condition: \"PTFE\", value: \"T90\"}]\n\n- Also connection_type axis: sensor_type 8711 → wafer; 8705 → standard flanges. Could be in models attributes or components characteristics. I'll put connection in model attributes? Since models are per sensor+DN, connection is determined by sensor type. I can add model_level attribute \"connection_type\" with values per model — string values. That's fine but verbose. Alternative: put it in models[].attributes. Hmm.\n\nLet me think about overall structure to balance completeness and length.\n\nPlan:\n1. certificate: number, page_total 7, member_state Czech Republic, date_issued 2011-12-19, revision 1? The schema doesn't have revision in certificate. revision_history covers it. oiml_issuer_id: not present. project_number: not present. scheme: not present → omit.\n\n2. issuing_authority: name, address_lines, person_responsible \"Jan Kalandra\", person_title? \"The Issuing Authority\" — his title line says \"The Issuing Authority\". Hmm, person_title could be omitted. Also Pavel Klenovský as CIML Member — not a schema field; could go in... there's no field. Omit or include in issuing_authority.person_responsible? The person responsible for the authority is Jan Kalandra. I'll omit Klenovský or mention in... maybe notes? No notes field. Omit.\n\n3. applicants: one entry. manufacturers: one entry.\n\n4. certified_type: category \"Water meter\", type_designations [\"8732\", \"8705\", \"8711\"], description (the measuring system description text, condensed).\n\n5. model_family: family_name null, models 28 entries with model_id like \"8711-DN4\" and attributes {sensor_type: \"8711\", nominal_dn: 4, connection_type: \"Wafer\", liner_material: [\"PTFE\"]...}. Hmm — putting attributes here AND model_level duplicates. The R137 example doesn't populate attributes. I'll keep models minimal: model_id only, maybe with attributes {sensor_type, connection_type} since those are constant within a sensor family and it avoids 28-value arrays for them. That's a reasonable compromise:\n   - models[].attributes: {sensor_type: \"8711\", connection_type: \"wafer\", liner_material: \"PTFE\"} for 8711; {sensor_type: \"8705\", connection_type: \"standard flanges\", liner_material: [\"neoprene\", \"polyurethane\", \"PTFE\"]} for 8705.\n   \n   Then model_level holds the varying numeric matrices.\n\n6. components: transmitter 8732 (role: measuring transducer with electronic calculator / transmitter), flow sensors 8705 OR 8711.\n\n7. characteristics:\n   type_level:\n   - q2_over_q1_ratio: 1.6\n   - q4_over_q3_ratio: 1.25\n   - environmental_classes: \"B and C\" → value string \"B and C\"? Could be list. I'll use value: \"B, C\" or \"B and C\" string.\n   - electromagnetic_environment: \"E1 and E2\"\n   - minimum_straight_length_inlet_pipe: 5 (dimensionless — multiple of DN) unit null\n   - minimum_straight_length_outlet_pipe: varies! 3 (8711 tables 1-2), 2 (8705 tables 3-5). Hmm, that varies by sensor type — model_level or config_level axis \"sensor_type\". I'll make it config_level: attribute minimum_straight_length_outlet_pipe, axis sensor_type: {condition \"8711\", value 3}, {condition \"8705\", value 2}.\n   - orientation: \"Arbitrary orientation\" → attribute orientation_limitation, value \"Arbitrary orientation\".\n   - indicating_range: varies by model/DN group — model_level.\n   - power supply: type_level attributes:\n     - ac_power_supply_voltage_max: 250 V\n     - ac_power_supply_voltage_min: 90 V\n     - ac_power_supply_frequency: {min:50, max:60} Hz (\"50 or 60\")\n     - dc_power_supply_voltage_max: 42 V\n     - dc_power_supply_voltage_min: 12 V\n     Or one power_supply attribute? Rules say split voltage/frequency/type. I'll do:\n     - power_supply_type_ac_voltage: {min 90, max 250} V? Actually Umax 250, Umin 90 → range 90-250 V. Good: power_supply_voltage (AC): {min: 90, max: 250}, unit V; power_supply_voltage (DC): {min: 12, max: 42} V. Two axes (AC/DC) — config_level axis \"power_supply_type\": condition AC → {min:90, max:250} V; condition DC → {min:12, max:42} V; and frequency config for AC 50-60 Hz. Or simpler as type_level attributes with prefixes. I'll use config_level:\n       - attribute \"power_supply_voltage_range\", axis \"power_supply_type\", values: [{condition \"AC\", value {min:90,max:250}, unit V}, {condition \"DC\", value {min:12,max:42}, unit V}]\n       - attribute \"power_supply_frequency\", axis \"power_supply_type\", values: [{condition \"AC\", value {min:50,max:60}, unit \"Hz\"}, {condition \"DC\", value null...}] — DC has no frequency. Hmm, awkward. Maybe simpler: separate type_level attributes:\n       - ac_supply_voltage_max: 250 V; ac_supply_voltage_min: 90 V; ac_supply_frequency: {min:50,max:60} Hz; dc_supply_voltage_max: 42 V; dc_supply_voltage_min: 12 V.\n       That's clean. I'll do that.\n   - accuracy_classes: in recommendation.accuracy_classes [1,2]; also maybe type_level accuracy_class? It varies by model → model_level.\n\n   model_level attributes (arrays over 28 models):\n   - nominal_dn (mm)\n   - overload_flowrate_q4 (m³/h) — max\n   - permanent_flowrate_q3 (m³/h) — max, fn 1\n   - transitional_flowrate_q2 (m³/h) — min\n   - minimum_flowrate_q1 (m³/h) — min\n   - q3_over_q1_ratio_max (fn 2)\n   - accuracy_class\n   - maximum_admissible_pressure (bar) range\n   - length_of_water_meter (mm)\n   - resolution_of_indicating_device (L)\n   - indicating_range (m³) — range\n\n   That's 11 × 28 = 308 entries. Long but OK. Actually, maybe I should reduce: indicating_range could be captured in matrix_tables only. And max_admissible_pressure... it varies (19/50/40). Keep it.\n\n   config_level:\n   - mpe attributes × accuracy_class axis (3 entries)\n   - temperature_class × liner_material axis (1 entry)\n   - minimum_straight_length_outlet_pipe × sensor_type axis (1 entry)\n\n8. matrix_tables: 5 tables, each with columns and rows. Columns: sensor? Each table: dn, q4, q3, q2, q1, q3_over_q1, q2_over_q1, q4_over_q3, accuracy_class, mpe_lower, mpe_upper_le30c, mpe_upper_gt30c, temperature_class, environmental_class, electromagnetic_environment, max_admissible_pressure, max_pressure_loss, indicating_range, resolution, min_straight_inlet, min_straight_outlet, orientation, length, connection_type, plus power supply (same for all). To keep tables manageable, I'll include the varying + key constant columns: dn, q4_max, q3_max, q2_min, q1_min, q3_over_q1_max, accuracy_class, mpe_lower, mpe_upper_water_le30c, mpe_upper_water_gt30c, max_admissible_pressure_bar, indicating_range_m3, resolution_l, min_straight_inlet_dn, min_straight_outlet_dn, length_mm. And put fully-constant rows (q2/q1, q4/q3, pressure loss, env classes, power) into description or omit from rows (they're captured in type_level). The matrix_tables rows should probably reflect the table as-is, but trimming fully-constant columns is acceptable as they're captured elsewhere. Hmm, \"preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\" — these tables DO fit into model_level. But the values like colspan groupings (Q3/Q1 per DN groups, accuracy class splits) are preserved in model_level anyway. Do I need matrix_tables at all then? The R137 example extracts both matrix_tables AND model_level. I'll include matrix_tables for fidelity — but that doubles the data again. \n\nLet me estimate size. Each matrix_table with 5-6 rows × ~16 columns. 5 tables ≈ 28 rows × 16 fields ≈ 450 values. Plus model_level 308 values. Total JSON maybe 2000+ lines if pretty-printed. That could be very long. The output has no strict length limit but I should be pragmatic. \n\nDecision: Include matrix_tables (5 tables) with the full per-DN rows (they're the authoritative source), and populate model_level with the key attributes. To control size, in matrix_tables I'll use compact column names and include all rows. Both representations serve different consumers. I think it's fine.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: maybe I can skip matrix_tables since model_level fully captures the per-DN variation, and matrix_tables is for things that \"don't fit elsewhere\". But the instruction example for R137 does both. The safest per instructions: do both. But the doc says matrix_tables preserve tables that don't fit elsewhere. Since these tables fit in model_level, arguably skip. However, there are subtleties (colspan groupings for Q3/Q1 and pressure) that are preserved in model_level too. Hmm.\n\nI'll include matrix_tables because the values encode ranges (≤/≥) and groupings cleanly, and it preserves the exact certificate layout. But to save space, I'll keep matrix_tables columns to the varying ones: dn, q4_max_m3h, q3_max_m3h, q2_min_m3h, q1_min_m3h, q3_over_q1_max, accuracy_class, max_admissible_pressure_bar, indicating_range_m3, resolution_l, length_mm. Constants (ratios, MPEs, env classes, straight lengths, power) go in type_level/config_level. That's a good balance.\n\nWait, but MPE varies per accuracy class per table — handled by config_level. Temperature class per liner — config_level. OK.\n\nRows in matrix_tables: values with ≤/≥ — I'll store as plain numbers in tables (with column names indicating max/min, e.g. q4_max). And footnote markers: Q3 rows have fn 1; Q3/Q1 fn 2. In matrix_tables, the schema has footnotes[] at table level. I can attach footnote markers... rows are plain objects; I'll note footnotes in the table-level footnotes array and add marker in column name or leave as is. I'll add \"footnotes\" listing both, and keep numeric values plain. Alternatively include footnote_markers in row objects like {\"q3_max\": 0.4, ...}. Schema says rows[] (object per row) — I can add a footnote marker key? I'll keep it simple: numeric values, plus table-level footnotes array with markers \"1)\" and \"2)\". And in model_level, footnote_markers on q3 values [\"1)\"] and q3_over_q1 [\"2)\"].\n\nHmm, attaching footnote markers to every one of 28 q3 values adds bulk. Maybe just include footnote_markers on the attribute level? The schema puts footnote_markers inside each value object. Ugh. 28 × {\"1)\"} adds ~28 short arrays. Acceptable? It's more correct. Let me include them. Actually, to save space, I could omit footnote_markers on every value and just have the footnotes defined globally with the note that q3 references footnote 1... but rule 7 says record marker on the value. I'll include them — it's mechanical.\n\nHmm, this is going to be a very long JSON. Let me just be systematic and write it out. Actually, let me reconsider the footprint: 28 models × (q3 + q1 + q2 + q4 + q3q1 + dn + class + pressure + length + resolution + indrange) ≈ 28 × 11 = 308 value objects, each ~40-60 chars. ~15KB. Plus matrix tables ~28 rows × 11 cols ≈ 310 values ~8KB. Plus rest ~4KB. Total ~30KB. Fine.\n\nLet me now write all the data carefully.\n\nModel list with attributes:\n\n8711 models (wafer, PTFE liner):\n- 8711-DN4: dn 4, q4max 0.5, q3max 0.4, q2min 0.00512, q1min 0.0032, q3q1max 125, class 2, press 0-19, indrange 9999-99999, res 0.01, len 55\n- 8711-DN8: dn 8, q4 2.0, q3 1.6, q2 0.0205, q1 0.0128, q3q1 125, class 2, press 0-19, ind 9999-99999, res 0.01, len 55\n- 8711-DN15: dn 15, q4 7.9, q3 6.3, q2 0.0504, q1 0.032, q3q1 200, class 2, press 0-19, ind 9999-99999, res 0.1, len 56\n- 8711-DN25: dn 25, q4 20.0, q3 16.0, q2 0.128, q1 0.08, q3q1 200, class 2, press 0-50, ind 9999-99999, res 0.1, len 57\n- 8711-DN40: dn 40, q4 50.0, q3 40.0, q2 0.320, q1 0.2, q3q1 200, class 2, press 0-50, ind 9999-99999, res 1, len 73\n- 8711-DN50: dn 50, q4 78.8, q3 63.0, q2 0.504, q1 0.315, q3q1 100, class 2, press